---
import SectionHeading from './SectionHeading.astro';

/** Quick facts rendered in the sidebar next to the summary. */
const facts = [
  { label: 'Experience', value: '5+ years in software testing' },
  { label: 'Certified', value: 'ISTQB Foundation Level' },
  { label: 'Core stack', value: 'Playwright · TypeScript · Selenium · Java' },
  { label: 'Also', value: 'REST Assured · SQL · Sentry & Grafana' },
  { label: 'Languages', value: 'Turkish · English · Spanish' },
];
---

<section class="section" id="about">
  <div class="container">
    <SectionHeading index="01" eyebrow="About" title="About me" />

    <div class="about">
      <div class="about__body">
        <p class="about__lead" data-reveal>
          I&rsquo;m a software test engineer with more than five years across
          manual and automation testing — API, UI and database layers — and I
          own quality through the whole SDLC, from requirement analysis to
          production monitoring.
        </p>

        <p data-reveal style="--reveal-delay: 80ms">
          Most of my automation is built with <strong>Playwright</strong> and <strong>TypeScript</strong>,
          or Java frameworks using <strong>Selenium</strong> and <strong>Cucumber</strong>,
          always on the Page Object Model so suites stay maintainable as the
          product moves. I push verification down to the <strong>API</strong> layer
          with <strong>REST Assured</strong> and Postman against Swagger specs,
          and reach for <strong>SQL</strong> when the truth lives in the database
          rather than the UI.
        </p>

        <p data-reveal style="--reveal-delay: 140ms">
          The part I care most about is what happens after release. I own live
          monitoring during cross-team go-lives, using <strong>Sentry</strong> and <strong>Grafana</strong> to
          catch real production errors and triage incidents fast — finding the
          root cause and getting things stable again. That feedback loop is also
          what tells me which regression scenarios are worth automating next.
        </p>

        <p data-reveal style="--reveal-delay: 200ms">
          I still value <strong>manual and exploratory testing</strong> —
          automation confirms what you already thought to ask, while exploration
          is how you find the questions worth automating. Risk-based, either
          way: fewer surprises in production, and a team that can ship without
          holding its breath.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="about__facts" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay: 120ms">
        <dl>
          {
            facts.map((fact) => (
              <div class="fact">
                <dt>{fact.label}</dt>
                <dd>{fact.value}</dd>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </dl>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .about {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.6fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .about__body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    max-width: 46rem;
  }

  .about__lead {
    font-size: clamp(1.0625rem, 1.8vw, 1.25rem);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .about__body strong {
    font-weight: 550;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  /* --- Quick facts --- */
  .about__facts {
    padding: 1.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    background: var(--bg-elev);
    position: sticky;
    top: calc(var(--header-h) + 2rem);
  }

  .fact + .fact {
    margin-top: 1.15rem;
    padding-top: 1.15rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .fact dt {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  .fact dd {
    margin: 0.4rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .about {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .about__facts {
      position: static;
    }
  }
</style>
